$ mcpdial call --help
exit 0
--- stdout
Call a tool

Usage: mcpdial call [OPTIONS] <TARGET> <TOOL> [ARGUMENTS]...

Arguments:
  <TARGET>
          A saved name, an http(s):// URL, or stdio:<command>

  <TOOL>
          A tool name from `mcpdial tools TARGET`

  [ARGUMENTS]...
          One JSON object (inline, @file, or - for stdin), or key=value pairs

Options:
      --elicit <JSON>
          Answers for anything the server elicits mid-call: a JSON object or @file

      --no-browser
          Print a url-mode elicitation's address instead of opening a browser

      --check <FILE>
          Refuse to call when TOOL has drifted from this snapshot; exit 3 with the differences

      --strict
          With --check: hold TOOL to the object the snapshot holds

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

Global options:
      --timeout <SECS>
          Seconds to wait for a reply; else the server's saved timeout, else 60. With `add`, saved. (MCPDIAL_TIMEOUT=SECS does the same)

      --json
          Emit JSON instead of a readable summary (MCPDIAL_JSON=1 does the same)

      --plain
          Print what a pipe would get, even at a terminal (MCPDIAL_PLAIN=1 does the same)

      --no-pager
          Print long output straight to the terminal instead of through a pager (MCPDIAL_PAGER= does the same everywhere)

      --raw
          Print a server's text as it was written instead of rendering its markdown, for copying it out of the terminal

      --color <WHEN>
          Colour the terminal output: `always` (for `less -R`), `never`, or `auto` (at a terminal, unless NO_COLOR is set)

          Possible values:
          - always: Even into a pipe or a file, for `less -R`
          - never
          - auto:   At a terminal, unless NO_COLOR is set
          
          [default: auto]

  -v, --verbose
          Trace every message on stderr (and pass a stdio server's stderr through)

      --progress
          Print a line on stderr for every progress notification a server sends during a call, whether or not stderr is a terminal

      --log-level <LEVEL>
          Show a server's own log notifications from this level up (default warning), and ask a server that supports logging to send no less

      --trace <FILE>
          Append every message and transport event to FILE as JSON Lines, secrets redacted (MCPDIAL_TRACE=FILE does the same everywhere)

      --user-agent <USER_AGENT>
          Override the User-Agent (HTTP only) (MCPDIAL_USER_AGENT does the same)

  -H, --header <'Name: value'>
          Extra HTTP header, repeatable. With `add`, saved to the server

      --token-env <VAR>
          Env var holding a bearer token; beats any saved credential. With `add`, saved. A `${VAR}` in any `-H` header value does the same for that header

      --protocol-version <VERSION>
          MCP protocol revision to speak, instead of working out which the server does. With `add`, saved

      --no-retry
          Fail on the first transient HTTP failure instead of sending the request once more

      --save-dir <DIR>
          Write image, audio and blob blocks to DIR/<tool>-<n>.<ext> instead of printing them (call, prompt, read, shell)

      --max-chars <N>
          Show at most N characters of a result; under --json a `truncated` object replaces it (call, prompt, read, raw, shell; MCPDIAL_MAX_CHARS=N does the same)

  -o, --output <FILE>
          Write the whole result to FILE, which must not exist, and print one line saying so (call, prompt, read, raw, shell)

      --no-daemon
          Dial a stdio server afresh even when `start` left one running (MCPDIAL_NO_DAEMON=1 does the same everywhere)
--- stderr
